WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.780
SIERRA: Hello out there. This episode was&nbsp;
produced in conjunction with CFR's "Women in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:11.940
Foreign Policy" program. They've been working for&nbsp;
years to elevate the status of women and girls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:17.940
and to make sure everyone is informed about&nbsp;
global issues related to gender equality. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:23.940
please, after the episode, check out their&nbsp;
page at cfr.org. You can find a whole range&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:28.500
of really amazing work, and I encourage&nbsp;
you to do it. These include their Women's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:33.600
Power Index and a new suite of content&nbsp;
about human trafficking. Okay, thanks!

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.780
TEEKAH: And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:46.740
it's such a visceral foundational thing that you&nbsp;
just want to have a good life. That's it. You just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:51.420
want to have a good life. And these are such basic&nbsp;
human rights that we're all entitled to. The right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:57.060
to life, the right to be free from sexual assault,&nbsp;
the right to be free from torture and confinement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:02.520
the right to a sustainable livelihood, the right&nbsp;
to decent working conditions and to respect.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:09.840
SIERRA: Human trafficking is more than&nbsp;
just a crime, it’s a process. A process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:16.560
that slowly deprives people of their humanity by&nbsp;
exploiting what is most human in them. The process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:22.440
is lucrative, and deeply ingrained in the global&nbsp;
economy. Experts estimate that there are between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:28.980
25 and 40 million victims of human trafficking&nbsp;
globally, and some say it’s one of the fastest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:33.900
growing aspects of transnational crime.
Today we’re going to talk about a segment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:39.840
of human trafficking known as labor trafficking.&nbsp;
And our primary focus is going to be the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:46.140
States - because this crime is happening everyday&nbsp;
right here, in our own cities and neighborhoods.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:50.340
I’m Gabrielle Sierra, and&nbsp;
this is Why It Matters. Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:53.760
what is labor trafficking,&nbsp;
and how can it be stopped?

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:01.680
ANDOLE:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:13.080
My name is Suzy Andole. So, when I was working in&nbsp;
Africa, I met a friend, uh, who is a nanny. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:19.080
when she mentioned about the job, I'm like, “Oh,&nbsp;
yes I will go.” I [was] supposed to work Monday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:28.740
to Friday to get $439 per week. God answered&nbsp;
my prayer, you know. I'm getting a better job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:32.160
better pay, for my better life, [for]&nbsp;
me and my daughter and my family.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:37.740
SIERRA: Susy Andole is a survivor of&nbsp;
human trafficking. She came to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:41.280
United States to work for a Somalian&nbsp;
family that sponsored her visa.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:45.900
ANDOLE: So, I went home, I came back to Nairobi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:54.060
they scheduled my flight. I came here in [the]&nbsp;
United States. You know I'm new. It was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.060 --> 00:03:00.240
little cold. In my country it's not cold.&nbsp;
I didn't know, you know, we are staying in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:07.980
the plane that long. My feet was all swell, you&nbsp;
know. I can't even fit in my shoe[s] and I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:15.540
wearing high heel[s] for 20 hours in the plane.&nbsp;
So, she came and pick me [up] at the airport.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:26.460
So, from there she brought me home. I'm so&nbsp;
tired. So, after that, I ask for food. She gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:31.920
me something little. I'm like, "oh my God. This&nbsp;
is not enough." So, my life started like that.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:38.100
SIERRA: The fact that her host family didn’t&nbsp;
feed her after a 20 hour flight was only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:42.900
the first sign that promises made to Susy in&nbsp;
Kenya would not be kept in the United States.

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:51.060
ANDOLE: I used to work 14 to 16 hours, sometimes&nbsp;
18 hours. When I started, let's say in October,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:56.760
November, I didn't get my salary. My first&nbsp;
salary was in December. Only $200. And she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:03.960
told me I still owe her some money because I'm&nbsp;
not paying anything. Like, I'm living for free:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:09.600
free food, free house, and I'm still paying the&nbsp;
[plane] ticket. She bought it for me to come over.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.200
SIERRA: Her legal documents were also withheld,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:15.860
and she was never allowed to&nbsp;
leave the house on her own.

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:21.840
ANDOLE: Things started changing. So, she&nbsp;
was telling me, "Oh, you can't keep money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:27.180
in the house." That time I don't have even&nbsp;
ID. I don't have even any of my documents.

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:27.750
SIERRA: Wow.

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:32.820
ANDOLE: She used to tell me, "If you&nbsp;
run, I will call the police. They will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:39.240
take you to jail. After jail, they will&nbsp;
deport you. I was stuck with no way out.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:47.600
SIERRA: So what actually defines&nbsp;
a victim of human trafficking?

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:52.380
TEEKAH: So there are different&nbsp;
legal frameworks that we can use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:56.220
when defining whether someone is a&nbsp;
victim of human trafficking or not.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:01.980
My name is Anita Teekah. I'm the senior director&nbsp;
of the anti-trafficking program at Safe Horizon.

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:07.380
SIERRA: Safe Horizon is a leading victim&nbsp;
services nonprofit based in New York.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:13.080
TEEKAH: But the most common that we use is the&nbsp;
federal trafficking victims protection act,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:17.640
which means legally that you have&nbsp;
to be a victim of either the use of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:22.020
force or fraud or coercion in order&nbsp;
to compel you to engage in any type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:25.320
of commercial sexual activity&nbsp;
or any type of forced labor.

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:31.920
SIERRA: While sex trafficking is a regular fixture&nbsp;
on the evening news or Law and Order episodes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:38.760
labor trafficking can be harder to see. But it&nbsp;
is happening. One of the main misconceptions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:43.440
about labor trafficking is that it’s mostly&nbsp;
about smuggling people across borders against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:48.560
their will. And while that does happen,&nbsp;
it’s not the crux of the issue at all.

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:54.660
TEEKAH: By and large, the majority of our clients&nbsp;
who are subsequently trafficked as domestic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:59.580
workers come here through the legal immigration&nbsp;
system on temporary nonimmigrant worker visas.

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:02.100
SIERRA: So they weren't being&nbsp;
trafficked at the time that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:04.860
they entered the country or at&nbsp;
least they weren't aware of it.

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:09.240
TEEKAH: Correct. And so what it looks&nbsp;
like is usually in the home country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:15.000
a recruiter is acting on behalf of a family&nbsp;
or on behalf of a company and they are making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:21.360
certain promises to interested applicants for&nbsp;
a domestic worker position. The recruiter will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:26.880
note the hours of work, the work conditions, the&nbsp;
pay. And then when the worker comes over here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:33.360
they encounter a situation that's very different,&nbsp;
whether it's working 18 hours a day, and not being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:39.660
given any breaks, not having adequate access to&nbsp;
food, not being allowed to take time off to see a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:44.820
physician if they're experiencing any injuries or&nbsp;
complaints. And so the promises that are made in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:49.040
the home country are often very different from the&nbsp;
reality encountered in the destination country.

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:55.020
LAGON: Trafficking sounds like it's about&nbsp;
movement, but it's really the human trade.

00:06:55.020 --> 00:07:01.080
SIERRA: This is Mark Lagon. He served as the&nbsp;
Director of the Office to Monitor and Combat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:06.540
Trafficking in Persons under President George&nbsp;
W. Bush, and was the Executive Director of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:12.500
Polaris Project, a global leader in the fight to&nbsp;
prevent modern-day slavery and human trafficking.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:19.740
LAGON: So people will tell poor people in&nbsp;
developing countries, neighboring countries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:23.940
you can have this great job, this&nbsp;
great experience, a better life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:29.700
You can send money back to your&nbsp;
family. And if you come work here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:36.060
either as a guest worker with papers&nbsp;
or coming, undocumented, we'll get you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:40.860
into a good job. And then they're lied to,&nbsp;
they're put into something really onerous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:47.160
nasty forced labor situation. And it turns out&nbsp;
to be the equivalent of modern day slavery.

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:51.960
SIERRA: And what are the recruiters or what are&nbsp;
these organizations getting in return for this?

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:56.580
LAGON: Money. I mean, the fact is that&nbsp;
it's worth thinking about the economics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.580 --> 00:08:03.000
of this. There are criminals, either small fry&nbsp;
criminals or big organizations and they choose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:09.660
human trafficking over arms trade or, you&nbsp;
know, drug trafficking, because there's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:13.680
maximum payoff and money and the minimum&nbsp;
chance that they're going to get caught.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:21.120
SIERRA: And who else is&nbsp;
benefiting from this process?

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:26.400
TEEKAH: Frankly, it's the family for whom&nbsp;
the domestic worker is working. If the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:31.440
domestic worker's coming over here as part of&nbsp;
a scheme that's perpetrated by a large company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:37.260
like a nursing home company or a home health&nbsp;
aide company, then they're obtaining really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:42.960
cheap labor from foreign countries. They&nbsp;
are targeting individuals who are already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:48.600
so incredibly financially unstable. They&nbsp;
are getting an incredible amount of work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:54.900
for free or for very low costs and the cost&nbsp;
that is paid by the worker is unfathomable.

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:59.340
SIERRA: So in some of these scenarios,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:04.080
is the host family even aware that&nbsp;
their domestic worker is trafficked?

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:09.960
TEEKAH: So it depends on how that domestic&nbsp;
worker came into that person's household,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:16.320
into the host families, sort of, orbit. And so&nbsp;
at times, yes that host family can know very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:20.700
well that they are not giving this person breaks,&nbsp;
that they're not paying them their hourly rate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:24.720
that they're not providing them with overtime,&nbsp;
that they are not providing them with access&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:29.820
to food and to medical care, and even if&nbsp;
the worker was originally recruited by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:34.920
third party intermediary, once that person&nbsp;
is in your home, you have an ethical and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:39.600
moral obligation to ensure that you're treating&nbsp;
them like a decent human being. And, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:46.200
it's funny because I don't know that employers in&nbsp;
the United States think that critically about it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:51.960
I think what they see is this is a cheaper form of&nbsp;
labor. With private workers, if you pay the worker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:56.460
under the table and so they're not declaring taxes&nbsp;
or if you're not paying your worker at all, you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.460 --> 00:10:01.860
not likely to announce that publicly or document&nbsp;
that anywhere. So we actually don't know how many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:07.260
privately employed domestic workers there are.&nbsp;
And so that's why it's so hard to estimate how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:12.360
prevalent human trafficking actually is because&nbsp;
with the numbers we do have, it's incredibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:17.520
pervasive. And so we know that like with any&nbsp;
other type of crime that's severely underreported,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:22.260
if we know of a thousand cases, there are&nbsp;
probably 10,000 cases that we don't know about.

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:25.560
SIERRA: While the exact numbers aren’t known,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:30.840
the issue has received a lot of attention&nbsp;
from lawmakers over the last two decades. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:34.980
fact it’s been a rare case of bipartisanship&nbsp;
across several presidential administrations.

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:44.880
LAGON: In the year 2000, the Trafficking Victims&nbsp;
Protection Act was passed. I played a small part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:50.760
as a Senate staffer. It was a wonderful example of&nbsp;
people on the left and the right coming together.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:59.040
That doesn't happen very often today. And that&nbsp;
legislation helped set up an apparatus to hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:05.400
the perpetrators of human trafficking to account&nbsp;
- prosecution, to help the victims - protection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:09.420
and to try and stop it from happening&nbsp;
in the first place - prevention.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:16.080
It also sets up provisions for the US&nbsp;
helping other countries by putting out a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:20.280
report to give grades of other countries&nbsp;
and give them advice and assistance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:24.840
I used to head the State Department&nbsp;
office that that legislation created.

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:27.720
SIERRA: And how successful has it been?

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:33.300
LAGON: In the United States there's been a&nbsp;
lot of progress. It's varied in the 50 States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:39.300
more of the progress has been made on sex&nbsp;
trafficking. The international labor organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:44.940
and a lot of experts on human trafficking have&nbsp;
found that over three quarters of the victims of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:52.860
human trafficking are exploited for labor rather&nbsp;
than exploited for sex. And so looking for those,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:58.800
finding those people, protecting them, giving&nbsp;
them shelter, giving them medical care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:08.280
giving them therapy and helping them get trained&nbsp;
for other work to reclaim their dignity, those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:13.320
labor trafficking victims, that's the frontline&nbsp;
of progress that we need to grapple with.

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:19.500
SIERRA: So, once someone's been identified&nbsp;
as a victim of human trafficking in the US,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:23.160
what tools exist to help them at this point?

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:29.100
LAGON: Well, first of all, if they're from another&nbsp;
country and they happen to be undocumented,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:35.700
but it's not their fault that they've been subject&nbsp;
to three words in the law and the UN treaty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:42.060
force, fraud and coercion. They have to&nbsp;
cooperate with law enforcement. If they talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:48.540
with law enforcement or an immigration official&nbsp;
and help share information about those who have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:54.540
been exploiting them, they can be given what's&nbsp;
called a T visa, T for trafficking. And they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:59.520
stay in the United States on that humanitarian&nbsp;
visa. They can even work. And they should be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:06.300
because they should be treated, not as someone&nbsp;
to deport, but someone to help. A Congressman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:12.480
at the time of the legislation, Lamar Smith was&nbsp;
so suspicious that people would lie and say they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:18.180
were human trafficking victims, that he put a&nbsp;
cap on the number of those humanitarian visas.

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:18.773
SIERRA: What's the cap?

00:13:18.773 --> 00:13:19.620
LAGON: The cap’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.340 --> 00:13:26.400
5,000 and never gotten close to it. It’s a&nbsp;
reasonable thing to have been put out there to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:32.600
reassure those skeptical about people who might&nbsp;
game the system. It hasn't proven to be needed.

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:37.800
SIERRA: Politicians of both parties have been&nbsp;
focused on human trafficking for decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:43.740
but experts say that the current framework is&nbsp;
still not helping the majority of victims. Part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:47.340
of the problem is that most victims&nbsp;
don’t understand their basic rights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.340 --> 00:13:50.820
much less specific mechanisms&nbsp;
like T visas that could provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:57.000
them with a path to freedom. 
 
SIERRA: What are the forces that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.000 --> 00:14:01.520
bind to someone in human trafficking? What&nbsp;
is it that keeps them stuck where they are?

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:11.520
LAGON: Intimidation and debt often. It is&nbsp;
often not the most desperately poor person,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:22.140
but someone who is in a very difficult position&nbsp;
economically who is lured into, you know, teenage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:31.620
prostitution or into migrant worker situations.&nbsp;
And then it turns out that in the process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.620 --> 00:14:38.280
they have put themselves deeply into debt for the&nbsp;
privilege of being put into a horrible situation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:48.120
They're afraid of not only surviving&nbsp;
this situation of exploitation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:54.360
but if they came forward, they'd&nbsp;
be blamed or sent home in shame.

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:58.560
SIERRA: Mark is talking about&nbsp;
something called “debt bondage”.

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:06.420
TEEKAH: Debt bondage is a form of human&nbsp;
trafficking. And so say for example, the host&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:11.460
family charges you $5000 for the plane ticket&nbsp;
and for the visa and for any other travel and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.460 --> 00:15:18.180
immigration related costs. You don't have $5,000.&nbsp;
And so we've had clients who have leveraged or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:24.060
sold ancestral land in their home countries. They&nbsp;
have sold everything they can. And they've also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.060 --> 00:15:30.420
secured loans, not only from local banks and&nbsp;
lenders, but also from external and extended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:36.180
circles of families and friends. And so now you&nbsp;
have this debt. Now you're told by your employer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:41.580
okay, you have to pay $500 a month for housing.&nbsp;
You have to pay for any food that you consume.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:46.860
Couple that with either not being paid at all&nbsp;
or being paid very, very little, there is no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:52.500
way you are ever going to pay off this debt. So&nbsp;
many of our clients have expressed incredibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.500 --> 00:15:58.680
deep feelings of shame, and remorse, and guilt.&nbsp;
Because if you've borrowed money from family and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:03.900
from friends, all of those individuals have a&nbsp;
financial and an emotional stake in your success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.900 --> 00:16:09.744
in the United States. It's an incredibly shameful&nbsp;
thing to have to admit to all of these people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.744 --> 00:16:15.960
have invested in you, people who couldn't really&nbsp;
afford to invest in you, that you in some sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:20.580
have failed. And we know that it's not a failure.&nbsp;
We know that they're actually victims of crime and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.580 --> 00:16:25.320
exploitation, but that's not how they perceive&nbsp;
themselves. They perceive themselves as gullible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.320 --> 00:16:31.020
or naive. And so they continue to stay in the&nbsp;
situation with the hopes of making some money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.020 --> 00:16:35.700
back so they can send some money back to their&nbsp;
families or back to their home country to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:44.340
paying down this debt. And so in addition to the,&nbsp;
the emotional sort of bondage and the debt bondage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.340 --> 00:16:50.820
it's also confiscating passports and documents.&nbsp;
If you don't have any of your identity documents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.820 --> 00:16:55.260
if you are kept under lock and key and you cannot&nbsp;
leave the house, if you don't speak the language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.260 --> 00:16:59.100
who are you going to talk to? So we've had&nbsp;
clients who've been trafficked from state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:02.820
to state and they don't even know what state&nbsp;
they're in. They don't know what city they're in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.820 --> 00:17:07.020
they don't know what resources are available&nbsp;
to them. You're operating completely blind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:12.660
And given what is covered in media around&nbsp;
the world with respect to the US immigration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.660 --> 00:17:18.960
system and our deportation scheme, it's not&nbsp;
unreasonable that a foreign national domestic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:23.820
worker would have a really tangible, credible&nbsp;
fear of being deported by an abusive employer.

00:17:23.820 --> 00:17:30.180
SIERRA: In telling her story, Susy cited&nbsp;
all of these fears. And another one, too.

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:37.020
ANDOLE: I wanted to run, to escape,&nbsp;
but we have this young little girl.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.020 --> 00:17:43.800
She wasn't even a year yet. So, if I look&nbsp;
back, I'm a mother. I have my own kid too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.580 --> 00:17:47.820
If you leave the house, who you're&nbsp;
going to leave the kids with?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.820 --> 00:17:52.560
All that hour in the house. Then when I'm&nbsp;
leaving, I'm running, I'm going where?

00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:54.000
SIERRA:
Right.

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:55.020
ANDOLE:
They will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.020 --> 00:17:58.080
still catch me where I am because&nbsp;
you don't know where you're landing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:02.340
you know. I will go run and go out there.&nbsp;
That person will report me to police.

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:07.260
SIERRA: Her first chance at any type of freedom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.260 --> 00:18:11.420
in the United States came when she&nbsp;
asked to attend religious services.

00:18:11.420 --> 00:18:18.240
ANDOLE: I told them, "Oh, I need to start going&nbsp;
off. I want to go to church." I'm a Christian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.240 --> 00:18:23.460
they were Muslim. She took me&nbsp;
to a church, a Lutheran church.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:30.060
When I went to that church, those church&nbsp;
members were so friendly. They were happy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.060 --> 00:18:35.520
to see me. They were all white, I was the black&nbsp;
one in that church. So, they come to talk to me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:40.800
Where are you from? I told them I'm from Kenya&nbsp;
and then one mentioned, "Oh, there's another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.800 --> 00:18:46.860
lady from Tanzania." I told her, "Yes, uh, I&nbsp;
don't have my ATM card, I don't have my ID,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:52.320
I don't have my passport. She's keeping all my&nbsp;
things." And the woman said, "That's abuse."

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:58.020
SIERRA: Susy had found a friend, someone&nbsp;
who started explaining her rights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.020 --> 00:19:02.100
and who soon found her additional&nbsp;
work in nearby New Rochelle. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:07.320
this initial moment of hope turned out to be&nbsp;
the first in a chain of new working abuses.

00:19:07.320 --> 00:19:14.400
Susy was eventually able to escape her first&nbsp;
employer. And yet afterwards she found herself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:19.980
in a series of homes where person after&nbsp;
person exploited her vulnerability. She&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.980 --> 00:19:25.320
eventually landed in the Bronx. There, someone&nbsp;
overheard her being abused by her latest employer.

00:19:25.320 --> 00:19:31.020
ANDOLE: She's yelling at me in our&nbsp;
language and this guy was American,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:39.960
he sensed something the way she was talking to&nbsp;
me. Like, “uh, do faster, do quickly.” So, in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:47.580
elevator he ask[ed] me, "Oh, uh, who are you to&nbsp;
them? Are you related?" I just started explaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:55.380
to him. He's like, "Oh, I have a big house. You&nbsp;
can come and clean." And then my first question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.380 --> 00:20:02.160
was that, "Are you going to pay me?" He say&nbsp;
like, "Yes. I will pay you $20 per hour." I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:09.060
so happy. And then I went there, but I'm coughing,&nbsp;
I'm sneezing, I'm so skinny, like you know. I’m,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.060 --> 00:20:14.340
my face, even if you look at me, you just&nbsp;
know right away she's going through something.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.300 --> 00:20:21.060
So, he told me, "Oh, I can't even let you&nbsp;
clean my house. You need to go to the doctor."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.180 --> 00:20:30.060
So, when I went to Montefiore Hospital,&nbsp;
I filled [out] the form. I didn't put the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.060 --> 00:20:33.960
address. Why? Because they told me&nbsp;
to not give their address anywhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:41.100
So, when my doctor call me and then he say,&nbsp;
"Miss Andole, uh, where do you live?" I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.100 --> 00:20:47.460
didn't want to give the address. So, he&nbsp;
looked at me and then he tell the lady,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.460 --> 00:20:54.120
we came with one lady from African Services who&nbsp;
took us to the hospital. He told him to go out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.840 --> 00:21:01.320
So, he told me, "Miss Andole, do you have any&nbsp;
problem? I want you to tell me and I won't tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:08.100
anybody. I want to see if I'm going to help you."&nbsp;
So, I started telling him I'm from Kenya. I came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:13.920
here to work, uh, with a diplomat as a nanny.&nbsp;
Since I came to this country, if I get sick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:21.480
I've been given Advil and Aleve. If I go to my&nbsp;
menstruation, I don't have even sanitary diaper. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:27.218
used to use my t-shirt. I started opening for him&nbsp;
because he told me he won't mention it to anybody.

00:21:27.218 --> 00:21:27.232
SIERRA: Right.

00:21:27.232 --> 00:21:31.080
ANDOLE: And then he said, "That's&nbsp;
abuse. Let me call Safe Horizon."

00:21:32.280 --> 00:21:36.660
SIERRA: Susy visited Safe Horizon’s&nbsp;
Anti-Trafficking Program. There,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.660 --> 00:21:40.460
she was finally in the hands of professionals&nbsp;
who could give her the help she needed.

00:21:40.460 --> 00:21:46.380
ANDOLE: So, the case worker ended up&nbsp;
calling me. When I went to the office,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.380 --> 00:21:52.920
like on the waiting room, when I saw her&nbsp;
coming. Like, the love she showed me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:56.619
like I feel like, this is&nbsp;
it now. This is, you know.

00:21:56.619 --> 00:21:56.632
SIERRA: Yeah.

00:21:56.632 --> 00:22:07.380
ANDOLE: I cried, she hugged me. I cried, I cried.&nbsp;
I wasn't crying because I'm in, uh... because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.380 --> 00:22:14.100
of the stress and what I'm going through. I was&nbsp;
like, now, I've found my sister. Like I find now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.100 --> 00:22:20.700
she is going to give me help, you know? She told&nbsp;
me, "Don't worry. We'll try our best to help you."

00:22:23.240 --> 00:22:24.240
SIERRA:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:30.240
Do you think that the system as it currently&nbsp;
exists is helping the majority of victims?

00:22:30.240 --> 00:22:35.700
LAGON: I wouldn't say that's helping the majority&nbsp;
of victims, it's helping more and more. One of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:35.700 --> 00:22:43.860
problems I must say, is the lack of good data.&nbsp;
It's clear since that legislation was passed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:52.020
20 years ago, that we've made great progress in&nbsp;
laws, finding more victims. But my sense is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:52.020 --> 00:22:56.700
there are many more out there who have been&nbsp;
victimized that are afraid to come forward.

00:22:56.700 --> 00:23:00.300
SIERRA: I mean, there's a lot of fear&nbsp;
in the idea of being in a situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.300 --> 00:23:05.160
where you have no power and then going&nbsp;
to let's say the police and trusting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.160 --> 00:23:09.120
that they will help you in the right&nbsp;
way when you actually don't even know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:12.900
let's say about a T visa or any of the&nbsp;
rights that you have in this country.

00:23:12.900 --> 00:23:17.340
LAGON: Yeah. And the exploiters reinforce&nbsp;
the notion, you can't come forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:17.340 --> 00:23:22.080
you're going to be blamed, you're going to&nbsp;
be treated like the criminal. And, and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:28.080
have a lot of trauma. They're subjected&nbsp;
to violence and intimidation and fraud.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:32.280
And one of the things when you find a human&nbsp;
trafficking survivor is you need to remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:37.080
not just shelter, not just physical&nbsp;
medical care, but deal with the trauma.

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:43.140
SIERRA: So, how are we finding&nbsp;
and prosecuting the perpetrators?

00:23:43.140 --> 00:23:51.540
LAGON: Well, take for instance a maid who is&nbsp;
confined to a home, beaten, she might flee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:51.540 --> 00:23:59.820
and then she might talk, to law enforcement or&nbsp;
immigration officials about, those she worked for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:59.820 --> 00:24:05.760
those who mistreated her, the recruiters back&nbsp;
in her home country, those who placed her in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:05.760 --> 00:24:10.860
the United States. And then law enforcement has&nbsp;
evidence. This one of the reasons why having a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:17.220
heart and helping the human trafficking victims&nbsp;
is not just, you know, kind of a, a do-good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:17.220 --> 00:24:23.840
social welfare policy, but it will help them be&nbsp;
one of the most valuable sources of evidence.

00:24:23.840 --> 00:24:30.560
SIERRA: What is the international&nbsp;
community doing together?

00:24:30.560 --> 00:24:38.220
LAGON: Good question. The international&nbsp;
community cooperates on trying to look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:38.220 --> 00:24:43.680
patterns of human trafficking. Those&nbsp;
who are illicitly recruiting people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:43.680 --> 00:24:51.720
the money flows. Countries are given advice by&nbsp;
the UN office on drugs and crime and the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:51.720 --> 00:24:57.540
States on how they can form better laws and much&nbsp;
more importantly how to implement those laws.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:58.140 --> 00:25:06.060
But we need to increase the intensity. Countries&nbsp;
notice. Wow, the United States is in our face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:06.060 --> 00:25:11.340
about this and offering us help on this. It&nbsp;
doesn't help their business interests. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:11.340 --> 00:25:17.280
this is what we should be building and restoring&nbsp;
our foreign policy around. A calling card for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:17.280 --> 00:25:24.980
America that has a heart, that shows that we will&nbsp;
use power to stop something truly destructive.

00:25:24.980 --> 00:25:30.540
SIERRA: What are your thoughts on how the current&nbsp;
administration is handling human trafficking?

00:25:30.540 --> 00:25:35.100
LAGON: I think there’s some very good&nbsp;
things going on in this era. There's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:35.100 --> 00:25:40.500
lot of interest in human trafficking at the&nbsp;
White House. The daughter of the president,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:40.500 --> 00:25:48.960
Ivanka Trump has shed light on it. I think the&nbsp;
president has, perhaps perverted the understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:48.960 --> 00:25:53.940
of human trafficking by suggesting that those&nbsp;
who are trying to smuggle people across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:53.940 --> 00:26:01.800
border are human traffickers and the answer is&nbsp;
higher walls. The answer to human trafficking is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:01.800 --> 00:26:07.560
not to think of the problem as people's movement&nbsp;
across borders. It's to look at the exploitation.

00:26:07.560 --> 00:26:12.420
SIERRA: There's been a lot of talk&nbsp;
for years about needing, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:12.420 --> 00:26:16.620
comprehensive immigration reform.&nbsp;
What is the relation between our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:16.620 --> 00:26:20.600
overall immigration policy and&nbsp;
the issue of human trafficking?

00:26:20.600 --> 00:26:26.280
LAGON: At the time the human trafficking&nbsp;
legislation was passed in 2000 and for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:26.280 --> 00:26:32.460
most part since then, there was an understanding&nbsp;
that human trafficking victims needed to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:32.460 --> 00:26:39.180
differentiated from the general issue of&nbsp;
people being undocumented. I think I myself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:39.180 --> 00:26:45.300
feel that there needs to be some sort of policy&nbsp;
of regularizing the status of those people who've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:45.300 --> 00:26:49.860
been here for years. We need to think about the&nbsp;
children of those who came to the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:49.860 --> 00:26:56.460
undocumented, those people who do decent work.&nbsp;
I think the first step towards really reducing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:57.060 --> 00:27:02.844
the flow of, you know, elicit migrants is&nbsp;
to deal with the situation of those who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:02.844 --> 00:27:08.580
have been here for a while. But separate from&nbsp;
that, we need to maintain that understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:09.240 --> 00:27:15.180
that there are those who come to the United&nbsp;
States either as documented guest workers or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:15.180 --> 00:27:22.560
undocumented, who are subject to force, fraud and&nbsp;
coercion. And they lose control of their life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:22.560 --> 00:27:27.360
and they need to be treated like they're&nbsp;
fellow human beings like you and me.

00:27:27.360 --> 00:27:28.920
SIERRA: Mm-hmm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:28.920 --> 00:27:34.220
It's kind of a, you know, low bar baseline ask&nbsp;
just to be treated like a fellow human being.

00:27:34.220 --> 00:27:40.380
LAGON: You know, I'm a Christian, uh, I, you know,&nbsp;
I believe all people are of equal value. They're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:40.380 --> 00:27:46.740
all, you know, formed in the image of the Lord.&nbsp;
People who are secular, uh, look to UN treaties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:46.740 --> 00:27:51.560
see the, you know, universal,&nbsp;
equal worth of people. Same idea.

00:27:51.560 --> 00:27:57.240
SIERRA: Do you think that this is a problem that's&nbsp;
just too big for the United States to solve?

00:27:57.240 --> 00:28:02.280
LAGON: There is no reason to think that&nbsp;
globalization automatically leads to there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:02.280 --> 00:28:09.840
needing to be slavery. It really isn't necessary.&nbsp;
And the cost of doing more to assist victims of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:09.840 --> 00:28:15.600
human trafficking, beef up law enforcement,&nbsp;
give technical assistance to other countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:15.600 --> 00:28:21.000
so they not only passed laws, but, improve&nbsp;
them, and implement them, that isn't all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:24.900
that expensive. Look, when I headed the human&nbsp;
trafficking office of the State Department,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:24.900 --> 00:28:33.240
we had $18 million to give to all countries in the&nbsp;
world to nonprofits or international agencies to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:38.040
try and help victims of human trafficking&nbsp;
and train people to fight the problem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:38.760 --> 00:28:46.200
When we spend billions of dollars for corporate&nbsp;
welfare, uh, for defense, which we need for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:46.200 --> 00:28:53.760
our country, for important economic relief for&nbsp;
our citizens, merely doubling that 18 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:54.360 --> 00:28:59.400
would be nothing. It's in our means to&nbsp;
put a real dent in this problem and to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:59.400 --> 00:29:03.960
be able to study whether the things&nbsp;
we're doing are making a difference.

00:29:05.060 --> 00:29:10.800
TEEKAH: Honestly, it comes down to love of&nbsp;
family. And people will do anything for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:10.800 --> 00:29:15.360
people that they care about. I have seen some of&nbsp;
our clients put up with some of the most horrific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:15.360 --> 00:29:21.720
sustained abuse for the possibility of making a&nbsp;
hundred dollars in a month that they can send to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:21.720 --> 00:29:28.140
their family so their child in Mexico or in Peru&nbsp;
or in the Philippines can go to school and get an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:28.140 --> 00:29:32.760
education and not have to do what their parent&nbsp;
is doing. And the tipping point for our clients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:32.760 --> 00:29:39.120
is when I'm making no more money, I'm not making&nbsp;
anything whatsoever and I continue to accrue this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:39.120 --> 00:29:44.880
debt that I can't ever pay off, I have to do&nbsp;
whatever I can to leave. We've had clients who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:44.880 --> 00:29:48.840
have just run out the front door, they had nothing&nbsp;
with them. They couldn't take their clothes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:48.840 --> 00:29:52.620
they didn't have their passport, they had&nbsp;
nothing. And they just tried to find their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:52.620 --> 00:29:57.420
way to the nearest police station. Or they would&nbsp;
talk to somebody at church and they would say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:57.420 --> 00:30:00.480
"Help me. This is what I'm going&nbsp;
through. Can you please get me out?"

00:30:00.480 --> 00:30:03.480
SIERRA: Wow. That is incredible. That must&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:03.480 --> 00:30:08.160
take just an unbelievable amount of&nbsp;
bravery to do something like that.

00:30:08.160 --> 00:30:11.640
TEEKAH: It does. And you know, it&nbsp;
doesn't end when the person is out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:11.640 --> 00:30:15.660
of their trafficking. That's honestly, that's&nbsp;
just the beginning of the rebuilding for them.

00:30:17.360 --> 00:30:23.400
ANDOLE: It wasn't [an] easy journey, but&nbsp;
I'm so happy where I am now. I'm still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:23.400 --> 00:30:28.359
with them. They help me. I have a green&nbsp;
card, they help me bring my daughter here.

00:30:28.359 --> 00:30:28.372
SIERRA: Wow.

00:30:28.372 --> 00:30:33.180
ANDOLE: To reunite with me. Now even&nbsp;
I can talk [about] it without crying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:33.180 --> 00:30:39.120
because through the help I get from Safe&nbsp;
Horizon. They bring [a] smile on my face.

00:30:39.120 --> 00:30:44.280
SIERRA: With proper help, Susy was able&nbsp;
to get out of trafficking and into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:44.280 --> 00:30:48.060
home where she has built a new life.&nbsp;
She has remained with Safe Horizon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:48.060 --> 00:30:51.020
and is working to help&nbsp;
those who are still trapped.

00:30:51.020 --> 00:30:53.700
SIERRA: I mean, when you were trapped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:53.700 --> 00:30:59.280
did you know that you could go to the U.S.&nbsp;
authorities and tell them that you were a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:59.280 --> 00:31:03.180
victim of human trafficking? Did you&nbsp;
know there were laws to protect you?

00:31:03.180 --> 00:31:09.660
ANDOLE: No, I didn't know because, why? How will&nbsp;
I know? Even if I know, you're going where? I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:09.660 --> 00:31:15.600
was new in this country. This was my first time&nbsp;
traveling, going far from my country. And it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:15.600 --> 00:31:20.700
not me alone. We have a lot of people going&nbsp;
through a lot and I was lucky they didn't,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:20.700 --> 00:31:25.740
like the man didn't rape me. Some they rape&nbsp;
them, some they do such a bad things to people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:25.740 --> 00:31:31.860
and people are shy. Like people are scared,&nbsp;
if I say, if I run, and then you look back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:31.860 --> 00:31:38.040
even if you do this, where are you going? You're&nbsp;
going to maybe kill yourself, commit suicide. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:38.040 --> 00:31:43.980
you're like, "Let me stay in this position&nbsp;
I am." You know? So, to me I would like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:43.980 --> 00:31:50.160
like if there's a way, these things have&nbsp;
to be on TV every day for people to learn.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:50.160 --> 00:31:54.900
People are going through hell out there, but&nbsp;
they don't, they don't know their rights.

00:31:56.660 --> 00:32:01.260
SIERRA: If you believe you have information&nbsp;
about a case of human trafficking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:04.620
or want to connect to human&nbsp;
trafficking services in your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:04.620 --> 00:32:12.960
area, please call the national human&nbsp;
trafficking hotline at 1-888-373-7888.&nbsp;

